  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q6-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Suppose $\\cos\\alpha=-\\frac13$ and $\\sin\\beta=\\frac23$ where $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are both obtuse. Find $\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$-\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}+2}{9}$.",
      "worked": "Both angles are obtuse, so sine is positive and cosine is negative.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin\\alpha&=\\sqrt{1-\\left(-\\frac13\\right)^2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt2}{3}\\\\\\cos\\beta&=-\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac23\\right)^2}=-\\frac{\\sqrt5}{3}.\\end{align*}$$\nUsing the sine addition formula:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)&=\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta+\\cos\\alpha\\sin\\beta\\\\\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)&=\\frac{2\\sqrt2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt5}{3}\\right)+\\left(-\\frac13\\right)\\frac23\\\\\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)&=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}+2}{9}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 64
  },
